  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 150 PM CDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\nFriday night through Tuesday:\n\nSynopsis: Near or slightly above normal temperatures are expected \nthrough the duration of the long term period. A pair of upper \ndisturbances are progged to move through the western Great Lakes \nSaturday-Saturday night & Monday-Monday night, providing larger \nscale forcing for periods of showers & embedded thunderstorms. Each \ndisturbance will be accompanied by an affiliated pairing of surface \nwarm & cold fronts, with seasonably muggy conditions anticipated in \nthe warm sectors building in between the boundaries. Currently \nlooking at the highest humidities being on Saturday \nafternoon/evening & Monday afternoon/evening, when southern \nWisconsin is forecast to be each in each respective warm sector. Not \nanticipating humidity reaching heat headline-level thresholds during \neither period. \n\nFriday night through Saturday night: First of two upper disturbances \nwill cross southern Wisconsin, bringing periods of showers and \nthunderstorms. Expecting an initial wave of activity Friday night \ninto early Saturday morning as a warm front & affiliated warm/moist \nadvection overspread the area, with more precip development possible \nSaturday afternoon/evening along the trailing cold front. Saturday \nafternoon/evening storm development will be contingent on sufficient \nsunshine & thermodynamic recovery in the wake of overnight/early \nmorning activity, with more details becoming apparent as finer scale \nmodel guidance arrives later in the week. Raw shear and precipitable \nwater values point toward some potential for organized storms with \nheavy downpours in any development, though whether or not favorable \nparameters will be realized remains to be seen. Will be watching \ntrends through the end of the week and providing updates. \n\nSunday night through Monday night: Next window of shower and storm \npotential arrives ahead of the next upper disturbance. Expecting \nsimilar system evolution to Friday night-Saturday night, with an \ninitial batch of warm advection showers & storms Sunday night/early \nMonday morning & cold frontal redevelopment Monday \nafternoon/evening. Like the weekend system, global ensembles point \ntoward sufficient deep layer shear & precipitable water values for \nmore organized storms with heavy downpours, with the realization of \nfavorable environmental conditions being dependent on still \nuncertain thermodynamics. Will be providing updates as trends\nclarify.\n\nQuigley",
